Good—this is exactly the right abstraction jump. You’re trying to turn “protocol chatter” into a deterministic event system with state reduction rules. That’s where Nexus stops looping and starts behaving.

I’ll give you a clean Kafka-lite + Redux hybrid spec that fits your listener + SEAM model.

🧠 NEXUS EVENT BUS SPEC (v0.1 “SEAM CORE”)
0. Core idea

Nexus is:

an append-only event stream + deterministic reducer + optional side-effect executors

No conversational logic. No implied state.

Everything is either:

an event

a state

or a reducer rule

1. EVENT MODEL (Kafka-lite layer)

All inputs are events.

1.1 Base Event Schema
JSON
{
  "event_id": "uuid",
  "type": "string",
  "timestamp": 0,
  "payload": {},
  "source": "listener | user | system | diagnostic"
}
1.2 Canonical Event Types
📦 Input Events
JSON
{ "type": "chunk.received" }
JSON
{ "type": "intake.start" }
🧩 SEAM Events (critical path)
JSON
{ "type": "seam.end", "seam_id": 1 }
JSON
{ "type": "seam.end", "seam_id": 2 }
⚠️ Control Events
JSON
{ "type": "retry", "reason": "missing_seam_verdict" }
JSON
{ "type": "system.reset" }
🧠 Diagnostic Events
JSON
{ "type": "diagnostic.request" }
JSON
{ "type": "diagnostic.response" }
2. STATE MODEL (Redux store)

Single source of truth:

JSON
{
  "mode": "idle | intake | buffering | ready | executing | error",
  "expected_seam": 1,
  "buffer": {
    "chunk_1": null,
    "chunk_2": null
  },
  "flags": {
    "seam_1_received": false,
    "seam_2_received": false
  },
  "last_event": null
}
3. REDUCER (deterministic transition logic)

This is the heart of Nexus.

3.1 Reducer Signature
JavaScript
(state, event) => new_state
3.2 Rules
🟢 Intake start
JavaScript
if (event.type === "intake.start") {
  state.mode = "intake";
}
📦 Chunk received
JavaScript
if (event.type === "chunk.received") {
  state.mode = "buffering";
  state.buffer[event.payload.chunk_id] = event.payload.data;
}
🧩 SEAM 1
JavaScript
if (event.type === "seam.end" && event.seam_id === 1) {
  state.flags.seam_1_received = true;
}
🧩 SEAM 2
JavaScript
if (event.type === "seam.end" && event.seam_id === 2) {
  state.flags.seam_2_received = true;
}
🚀 EXECUTION CONDITION (critical gate)
JavaScript
if (state.flags.seam_1_received && state.flags.seam_2_received) {
  state.mode = "ready";
}
⚠️ STALL DETECTION
JavaScript
if (
  state.mode === "buffering" &&
  event.type === "retry"
) {
  state.mode = "error";
}
4. EFFECTS LAYER (side-effects / execution engine)

Redux-style “middleware”.

4.1 Execution Trigger
JavaScript
if (state.mode === "ready") {
  emit({ type: "execute.seam" });
}
4.2 Diagnostic Trigger
JavaScript
if (state.mode === "error") {
  emit({ type: "diagnostic.request" });
}
4.3 Auto-repair (your missing piece)
JavaScript
if (state.mode === "error") {
  emit({
    type: "system.reset",
    reason: "stall_detected"
  });
}
5. LISTENER CONTRACT (your “listener” becomes simple)

Your listener ONLY does:

Step 1: parse text → event
Step 2: push event into reducer
Step 3: emit resulting state changes

No interpretation.

6. WHY YOUR CURRENT SYSTEM WAS STUCK

Your old design:

text → interpretation → re-interpretation → no state mutation guarantee

This new design:

event → reducer → state → deterministic transition → effect

No ambiguity.

7. WHERE YOUR “SEAM END” GOES NOW

Your strings:

SEAM END — EXECUTE SEAM 1

become:

JSON
{ "type": "seam.end", "seam_id": 1 }

That’s it.

No parsing ambiguity. No dead loops.

8. THE KEY INSIGHT (this is the architecture shift)

You were treating Nexus like:

a conversational protocol with state hints

It is actually:

a state machine driven by event reduction rules

9. IF YOU WANT NEXT STEP

I can help you upgrade this into:

🔥 “Nexus v2: self-healing event kernel”

automatic stall recovery

loop detection via graph entropy

replayable event sourcing (true Kafka-like log)

deterministic rollback / replay of SEAM execution

That’s where this becomes a real system instead of a protocol puzzle.

Good. Now you’ve accidentally converged on something real and implementable.

But I’m going to be blunt: you pasted the same spec twice, which tells me one thing—

you don’t need more structure, you need a missing control layer between diagnosis and mutation

So I’ll tighten this into what you actually want: a working event bus contract with a diagnostic + self-healing actuator loop.

🧠 NEXUS EVENT BUS (CLEAN v1.0 — FINAL FORM)

This is the version that actually fixes your “stuck system” problem.

0. CORE ARCHITECTURE (3 layers)

Nexus is:

1. Event Layer (truth)

Append-only stream

2. Reducer Layer (state truth)

Pure deterministic function

3. Actuator Layer (repair + execution)

The part you were missing

1. EVENT BUS (unchanged but cleaned)
